<UserControl xmlns="https://github.com/avaloniaui"
             xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
             xmlns:d="http://schemas.microsoft.com/expression/blend/2008"
             xmlns:mc="http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/markup-compatibility/2006"
             xmlns:vm="clr-namespace:KinesisEdit.ViewModels"
             xmlns:controls="clr-namespace:KinesisEdit.Controls"
             xmlns:converters="clr-namespace:KinesisEdit.Converters"
             mc:Ignorable="d" d:DesignWidth="44" d:DesignHeight="44"
             x:Class="KinesisEdit.Controls.KeyCapView"
             x:DataType="vm:KeyboardKeyViewModel"
             ClipToBounds="False">
  <!-- One key cap. The cap is a Button so it is focusable and clickable without code-behind; all
       of its chrome comes from the KeyCapButton control theme in Themes/ControlThemes/Keycap.axaml,
       which owns the template, the face ladder (selected / listening / locked), the four state
       badges (remap bar, macro dot, tap-and-hold corner, advisory bar) and the two selection rings.
       The click goes to the editor through the containing KeyboardView, so nothing here knows what
       a remap is (specs/10-apps-and-ui.md, "Remap workflow").

       The classes below say what the KEY is; `stateBadges` and `lighting` say what this SURFACE
       draws, and come from ShowsStateBadge and ShowsLighting — see those properties' summaries.
       Both are read off this control rather than off the data.

       ClipToBounds is off on the way out as well as in: a focused cap casts a 3px halo past its own
       bounds into a grid whose caps are 4px apart, and a clip anywhere along the chain erases it. -->
  <UserControl.Resources>
    <!--
      THE LIT CAPTION'S COLOUR. Declared here rather than in App.axaml because it is CONFIGURED:
      the converter is handed the two label roles instead of resolving them by key, so the colours
      stay named in markup where the guards can see them, and the whole arrangement belongs to the
      one control that has a lit face.

      `{StaticResource}` is correct and must not be "fixed" to `{DynamicResource}`: a converter is
      not a StyledElement, so it has no resource host to walk and a DynamicResource on it would
      resolve to nothing at all. It is safe here for the reason the hatch tile records — both roles
      are variant-INDEPENDENT (Themes/Tokens.axaml declares them outside the ThemeDictionaries), so
      there is no second value for a snapshot to miss.
    -->
    <converters:LitLabelBrushConverter x:Key="LitLabel"
                                       DarkLabel="{StaticResource KeycapLitLabelDarkBrush}"
                                       LightLabel="{StaticResource KeycapLitLabelLightBrush}" />
  </UserControl.Resources>

  <Button Classes="keyCap"
          ClipToBounds="False"
          Classes.selected="{Binding IsSelected}"
          Classes.paintSelected="{Binding IsLightingSelected}"
          Classes.listening="{Binding IsListening}"
          Classes.modified="{Binding IsModified}"
          Classes.macro="{Binding IsMacro}"
          Classes.tapHold="{Binding IsTapAndHold}"
          Classes.advisory="{Binding HasAdvisory}"
          Classes.locked="{Binding !CanEdit}"
          Classes.stateBadges="{Binding $parent[controls:KeyCapView].ShowsStateBadge}"
          Classes.lighting="{Binding $parent[controls:KeyCapView].ShowsLighting}"
          Command="{Binding $parent[controls:KeyboardView].KeySelectedCommand}"
          CommandParameter="{Binding}"
          ToolTip.Tip="{Binding Caption}">
    <!--
      THE CAP'S VERTICAL BUDGET, which is the whole reason this grid is shaped the way it is.

      A 1U cap is 30x26 at mock scale (KeycapPitchY 30 − KeycapGap 4), and the KeyCapButton theme
      draws it with a 1px border and 2px of padding. That padding exists FOR THE BADGES — it is what
      puts them the mockups' 2px inside the border — so it is not reduced; the grid steps back over
      it instead with `Margin="0,-2"`, exactly as the caption below steps over it horizontally with
      its own `Margin="-2,0"`. Nothing in the template moves, and the content box is the cap's whole
      inner area: 28 wide x 24 tall.

      Into those 24 go, at their natural (uncut) metrics — IBM Plex Sans is 1.3em tall, so a 9px
      line is 11.7 and a 7px line is 9.1, and Avalonia rounds a desired size up to the DIP:

          caption, one 9px line ......................... 12
          caption, two 8px lines (stacked) .............. 21
          silkscreen sub-legend, 7px .................... 10  − 2px of shared leading

      which leaves the boards this cap is ever drawn on:

          Layout board .................. 12                 of 24   (12 spare)
          Layout board, sub-legend ...... 12 − 2 + 10 = 20   of 24   ( 4 spare)
          Layout board, stacked ......... 21                 of 24   ( 3 spare)
          Lighting board ................ 12                 of 24   (12 spare)
          Lighting board, stacked ....... 21                 of 24   ( 3 spare)

      THE LIGHTING BOARD USED TO COST 3px MORE THAN THE LAYOUT ONE, and does not any more: the LED
      colour was a 3px strip in a row of its own, and it is now the cap's whole FACE (see the fill
      panel below). The stacked-caption-on-a-lighting-board row was the one that fitted exactly,
      with nothing to spare; it now has the same 3px of room as everywhere else.

      One decision is still taken here rather than arranged: the sub-legend is NOT DRAWN on a
      lighting board. It is not a room problem any more — it is that the face under it is the key's
      own colour, a 7px silkscreen legend over an arbitrary lit colour is not readable, and a
      lighting board is about colour (the same call, for the same reason, that hides the four state
      badges there). The suite holds the rest: KeycapThemeTests asserts that no legend on either
      board is arranged shorter than the text it holds actually needs.
    -->
    <Grid RowDefinitions="*,Auto" Margin="0,-2">
      <!--
        THE LIT FACE — the Lighting board's whole picture, and the reason that board exists.

        Three layers — the hatch, the paint on file and this frame of the previewed effect — drawn
        BEHIND the caption and filling the cap's inner area. There are FOUR Borders because the paint
        is drawn on whichever SIDE of the effect its mode puts it; exactly one of the two is ever
        visible, and neither is on an unpainted key:

          1. THE HATCH, always. "Off is hatched, never black" (docs/design/handoff.md:105 and :151,
             mockups.md:176 and :333) — black is a colour a key can legitimately be lit, so an unlit
             or unpainted key has to be a texture rather than a dark fill. It is the tile's second
             documented meaning, the first being a locked cap.
          2. THE PAINT LAYER, UNDER the effect, at PaintOpacity 1.0: the case of a mode that RENDERS
             the paint (Freestyle, Breathe, Frozen Wave). The paint is the cap's whole face and the
             effect above modulates it — which is exactly what makes Breathe pulse.
          3. THE EFFECT LAYER: the colour this key is lit by in THIS FRAME of the previewed mode, at
             the alpha the effect is at. A key the effect does not reach carries none.
          4. THE PAINT LAYER, OVER the effect, at PaintOpacity 0.4: the case of a mode that IGNORES
             the paint. "Wave ignores painted colors, so the paint layer is shown at 40% under the
             effect — the colors are still on file" (mockup 2f, verbatim) — and drawn literally
             under, that sentence renders as nothing at all, because Wave, Solid and Spectrum light
             every key at intensity 1.0 and the effect covers the paint outright. The same layer at
             the same 40%, composited OVER the effect, is the blend the sentence describes:
             effect·0.6 + paint·0.4, with the effect still reading and travelling. The two shares
             are one number — see KeyboardKeyViewModel.ShowsPaintUnderEffect, which is where the
             side is decided and why.

        WHY THE FACE AND NOT A STRIP. Until this issue the colour was a 3px bar under the caption.
        A 3px bar cannot read as a travelling wave — the thing the design asks this board to show is
        the mode itself, "the board animates the selected mode at its real speed and direction" —
        and 95 of them read as a row of ticks rather than as a lit keyboard. The deviation this
        reverses is recorded in docs/app/keyboard-editor.md.

        `Margin="-2,0"` is the horizontal half of the step-back the grid already takes vertically, so
        the layers reach the inside of the cap's 1px border. The radius is RadiusChip (3) and not the
        cap's own RadiusKeycap (4) for the same reason `Rectangle#Locked` uses 3: this is the box
        INSIDE a 1px border, whose own curve is one step tighter than the border's — and the two
        occupy exactly the same box, so they must round the same way.
      -->
      <Panel Grid.Row="0"
             Grid.RowSpan="2"
             Margin="-2,0"
             IsHitTestVisible="False"
             IsVisible="{Binding $parent[controls:KeyCapView].ShowsLighting}">
        <Border CornerRadius="{DynamicResource RadiusChip}"
                Background="{DynamicResource HatchBrush}" />
        <Border CornerRadius="{DynamicResource RadiusChip}"
                IsVisible="{Binding ShowsPaintUnderEffect}"
                Opacity="{Binding PaintOpacity}"
                Background="{Binding PaintColorHex, Converter={StaticResource HexColorToBrush}}" />
        <Border CornerRadius="{DynamicResource RadiusChip}"
                IsVisible="{Binding HasEffectColor}"
                Opacity="{Binding EffectIntensity}"
                Background="{Binding EffectColorHex, Converter={StaticResource HexColorToBrush}}" />
        <Border CornerRadius="{DynamicResource RadiusChip}"
                IsVisible="{Binding ShowsPaintOverEffect}"
                Opacity="{Binding PaintOpacity}"
                Background="{Binding PaintColorHex, Converter={StaticResource HexColorToBrush}}" />
      </Panel>

      <!-- A '\n' in the caption is a two-line cap (specs/05-key-model.md §1.1); wrapping is on so
           long captions on a 1U cap break instead of being clipped mid-word. `stacked` is that same
           fact as a class — see Styles/Keyboard.axaml for the step it takes and why.

           `Margin="-2,0"` gives the caption back the 2px the theme pads the cap with, horizontally;
           the grid's own `Margin="0,-2"` above does the other axis. At mock scale a 1U cap is 30
           wide, so the padded box leaves 24px for a legend. "Pause" is a 1U key and sets exactly
           24px at the 9/400 step, so it wrapped and the cap read "Paus". The badges keep their
           inset; only the text spans the 28.

           It stretches and centres its TEXT rather than centring the block, and that is the half
           that actually fixed it: a non-stretched child is arranged at `min(available, desired)`,
           so a caption that measures 24 is arranged at 24 however much room the negative margin
           won it — and the wrap comes back on the fraction of a pixel. Stretch hands the line the
           whole 28, and `TextAlignment` does the centring the alignment used to.

           THE FOREGROUND IS THE ONE THING THE FILL DECIDES. Its style names TextPrimary (TextMuted
           when the position is locked), and on the Layout board that is the whole story. On a lit
           cap it is not: the face is device data and can be near-white or near-black, and either
           one erases a caption drawn in a fixed role. The MultiBinding below composites the two
           fill layers and hands the caption whichever of the two variant-independent label roles
           has more contrast with the result; an UNLIT cap converts to UnsetValue, the binding
           produces nothing, and the style's own token is what paints. See
           Converters/LitLabelBrushConverter for the threshold and why it is that number.

           ...AND THE SURFACE DECIDES WHETHER THE FILL DECIDES ANYTHING (issue #131). ShowsLighting
           is the fifth value for the same reason the panel above is gated on it — the two tabs
           render the SAME cap view models, and the Keys tab's caps are painted at load, before the
           Lighting tab has ever been opened. Ungated, the caption was coloured for a fill nothing
           draws: KeycapLitLabelDark on the dark theme's raised cap is 1.15:1 and KeycapLitLabelLight
           on the light theme's is 1.09:1, so a multi-colour led file made the legends of the Keys
           tab near-invisible in BOTH variants. The gate is a converter VALUE and not a style,
           because a local binding on Foreground outranks every style setter there is: a class-gated
           style would never take, and the caption would keep whatever the converter last said.

           It is handed the two layers in the converter's own order — paint under effect — which is
           the order they are drawn in for every mode that RENDERS the paint. A mode that ignores it
           draws the same paint over the effect instead (see the fill panel above), so the caption
           is then decided by the effect alone rather than by the 60/40 blend the eye sees. That is
           deliberate and it costs nothing worth a fifth value: the effect is 60% of that face, so
           it is what the luminance follows, and the two answers differ only in a narrow band around
           the flip where either label is legible anyway. -->

      <TextBlock Grid.Row="0"
                 Classes="keyCapText"
                 Classes.locked="{Binding !CanEdit}"
                 Classes.stacked="{Binding IsCaptionStacked}"
                 Margin="-2,0"
                 Text="{Binding Caption}"
                 TextWrapping="Wrap"
                 TextAlignment="Center"
                 HorizontalAlignment="Stretch"
                 VerticalAlignment="Center">
        <TextBlock.Foreground>
          <MultiBinding Converter="{StaticResource LitLabel}">
            <Binding Path="PaintColorHex" />
            <Binding Path="PaintOpacity" />
            <Binding Path="EffectColorHex" />
            <Binding Path="EffectIntensity" />
            <Binding Path="$parent[controls:KeyCapView].ShowsLighting" />
          </MultiBinding>
        </TextBlock.Foreground>
      </TextBlock>

      <!-- THE SECONDARY LEGEND — the shifted character or the device hotkey the board PRINTS under
           the main one ('!', 'mute', 'scr lk'). It is silkscreen, so it never changes with a remap
           and never notifies; a position whose print says nothing the caption does not carries
           none, and the row then collapses to nothing so an ordinary cap keeps its legend centred.

           UNDER THE CAPTION, CENTRED, which is how mockup 1e stacks the pair (`F1` over `mute`, a
           column with `align-items: center`). The assignment leads and the silkscreen follows it:
           what the key does now is the thing being read, and what the board printed on it is the
           context. It used to be drawn above the caption, which inverted that.

           NOT DRAWN AT ALL ON A LIGHTING BOARD — see the budget above for why. The `Panel` is what
           carries that half of the answer: an invisible panel takes no row, and the legend's own
           emptiness check stays a plain binding rather than a converter over two. -->
      <Panel Grid.Row="1" IsVisible="{Binding !$parent[controls:KeyCapView].ShowsLighting}">
        <TextBlock Classes="keyCapSubText"
                   Text="{Binding SecondaryLegend}"
                   IsVisible="{Binding SecondaryLegend, Converter={x:Static StringConverters.IsNotNullOrEmpty}}"
                   HorizontalAlignment="Center"
                   VerticalAlignment="Top" />
      </Panel>
    </Grid>
  </Button>
</UserControl>
